# A small JEPA world model on a low-cost arm: the encoder reads the outcome, the predictor adds nothing to it

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics, Paris. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

We trained a small JEPA world model in the LeWorldModel recipe for about ${{cost_round}} of rented GPU time. The training data are teleoperated demonstrations of one tabletop task on an SO-101 arm. We then re-tested on this model the uses of such models that recent papers propose. Each use had a threshold fixed before its test. We tested the encoder on policy rollouts the model never saw. A linear probe on its last-frame latent separates success from failure about as well as a hand-built colour judge. It does better than a frozen DINOv2 encoder and better than the commanded joints. The predictor adds nothing to this. At the grasp, the imagined latent reads the outcome at {{c1_imagined}} and the current latent at {{c1_current}}. Every use that needs the predictor to roll forward misses its threshold. It still does when we train the same recipe on 45 to {{sc5_eps}} episodes, which adds a second dataset and then another lab's rollouts. The controls are exploratory and come from one rig and one model per size.

## Introduction

![What the world model reads and what it forecasts. The encoder's last-frame latent separates success from failure on rollouts the model never saw about as well as a hand-built colour judge, and it keeps doing so when we retrain on 45 to {{sc5_eps}} episodes. The predictor misses its threshold at the original size and at every other size: the commanded actions rank the executed chunk better than the imagined latents do.](fig/encoder_predictor.pdf){#fig:teaser}{full}

LeWorldModel (LeWM) [@maes2026lewm] trains a world model in two parts. The encoder maps camera frames to a latent vector. The predictor takes the current latent and a sequence of actions and imagines the latents that follow. Both are trained together from pixels with a joint-embedding predictive (JEPA) loss. Most of the uses that later papers propose need the predictor. LeWM itself ranks a policy's candidate action chunks by their imagined outcome. ContactGuard [@zheng2026contactguard] reads the outcome of a grasp before it happens, HWM [@zhang2026hwm] and H-JEPA [@zhang2026hjepa] plan over several time scales, and other papers flag a failure when the observed latent drifts from the predicted one. Each of these papers reports that its use worked on its own robot and data.

We had one such model, trained on our own arm. We ran every use we could find on it, {{rows}} tests in all. {{n_paper}} of them re-test a claim of a paper, and {{n_own}} are uses we designed ourselves on the same model. One test is not a use of our model: it times the pretrained RoboJEPA [@zholus2026robojepa] and V-JEPA 2.1 [@murlabadia2026vjepa21] encoders on our on-board computer. {{n_fail}} of the {{rows}} tests fail, {{n_hold}} hold and {{n_np}} are not proven. Table @tab:tests groups them by what they ask of the model.

The first version of this paper argued something completely different. We read the failures as a sign that the latent mostly encodes the arm's pose. Then a frozen image encoder or the commanded joints would do at least as well. We ran these controls on {{n}} policy rollouts. The encoder read how the episode went better than either control. We modified the claim around the predictor.

We come back several times to one moment, the grasp anchor. It is {{anc_lead}} frames before the gripper first closes. There the current latent reads the outcome at {{c1_current}} and the imagined latent at {{c1_imagined}}.

The threshold of each test was fixed before its result; for the two-level progress test it was written down together with the result. The controls that split the encoder from the predictor were added after the data and had no threshold, so everything this paper says about that split is exploratory.

## Setup

The robot is one SO-101 arm [@so101] driven through LeRobot [@cadene2024lerobot], with a top and a wrist camera; it runs on position control, so the arm goes close to where it is commanded. The task is to put a bowl into another bowl. We trained our model on `ravediamond/so101_bowls_all_trimmed`, {{teleop_eps}} teleoperated episodes of about {{tel_ep_s}} s each ({{tel_min}} minutes in all), split by grid layout into {{train_eps}} training and {{val_eps}} validation episodes. The evaluation rollouts come from a separate dataset, `ravediamond/so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`. They are SmolVLA [@shukor2025smolvla] runs recorded after our model was trained: {{n}} episodes, {{success}} successes and {{failure}} failures over {{sessions}} sessions. Most failure monitors use the main set of {{ro_episodes}} episodes ({{ro_success}} successes, {{ro_failure}} failures), which leaves out the episodes of one policy family. The author ran the arm and labelled every outcome, and was not blind to the policy.

These data limit what the tests can show. The task is short and has no long horizon, no memory and no multi-step structure. Everything was recorded in one place, with one arm and one task family. The main set has {{ro_failure}} failures, so one episode can move a monitor's verdict. Every test ran offline on recorded episodes, never in closed loop on the arm.

Our model follows the LeWM recipe [@maes2026lewm], with a multi-view ViT encoder, an action-conditioned predictor and the SIGReg regulariser. We compare it with four cheaper signals. The frozen DINOv2-S control is a frozen DINOv2 small encoder [@oquab2023dinov2], mean-pooled over the top and wrist views as in DINO-WM [@zhou2024dinowm]. The joints control is the commanded joint vector. The colour judge is a hand-built rule on the final frame with no learning. Policy identity is the checkpoint that ran the episode.

One timing test loaded a random encoder by mistake, because a library version silently dropped {{audit_dropped}} encoder tensors; we voided its latents, and every other test ran the trained model (Appendix B).

## What we tested

Table @tab:tests sorts the {{rows}} tests by what they ask of the model, and Table @tab:register in the appendix gives each one with its threshold and result. Each group has a cheap competitor that needs no world model, run on the same episodes. Where we compared a method with its competitor, a paired bootstrap over episodes ({{bs_resamples}} resamples, 95 % interval) says whether the difference could be luck: an interval entirely below zero means the method lost.

<!-- tests -->

The cheap competitor is strong on this rig. The arm runs on position control, so the commanded joints already say where it will end up. The task is short, and little else in the scene moves. A learned latent has little room to add to what the commands carry. On a long task, or with a compliant arm or moving objects, we would expect this competitor to be weaker.

Each failure is also labelled with whose method failed (Table @tab:register, the same labels as our re-test of robot-learning papers on this rig). The authors' method, or the authors' method with only what our rig forced changed, counts against the paper's claim on this rig. Our variant of a method, a test outside the paper's regime, and a use of our own do not.

## What the encoder knows

Among the encoder uses, reading the outcome works. The outcome readout at rest passes. At the grasp anchor the current latent reads the grasp at {{c1_current}}. Reading progress does not work. Four uses ask the latent for progress or task stage, and all four fail. They are progress monotonicity, two-level hierarchical progress [@zhang2026hjepa], the latent stage tracker and SURVEYOR's latent-distance scale [@surveyor2026]. We also ran the pose probes of LeJEPA [@klindt2026lejepa]. A linear readout recovers about half of the joint variance from our encoder (R² {{lejepa_w1}}). It recovers more from the frozen DINOv2-S control ({{lejepa_w2p}}).

We tested whether the encoder carries anything that a frozen encoder or the joints do not. Table @tab:controls sets our model beside the four other signals on the same {{n}} rollouts. Every probe is the same leave-one-session-out logistic regression, fit against the author's labels (Appendix C). At rest the interval is a 95 % episode-bootstrap CI. At the anchor it is a bootstrap of the mean over 5 seeds of the out-of-fold AUROC, with episodes resampled jointly across the seeds (2,000 resamples, percentile).

Table @tab:controls: Outcome AUROC of each signal on the same {{n}} rollouts (exploratory: controls chosen after the data). No judge applies at the anchor.

| feature | at rest | grasp anchor ({{anc_scored}} scored, {{anc_fail}} fail) |
|---|---|---|
| our world model | {{rest_W1}} [{{rest_W1_lo}}, {{rest_W1_hi}}] | {{anc_W1}} [{{anc_W1_lo}}, {{anc_W1_hi}}] |
| colour judge (no learning) | {{rest_judge}} [{{rest_judge_lo}}, {{rest_judge_hi}}] | — |
| frozen DINOv2-S | {{rest_W2p}} [{{rest_W2p_lo}}, {{rest_W2p_hi}}] | {{anc_W2p}} [{{anc_W2p_lo}}, {{anc_W2p_hi}}] |
| commanded joints | {{rest_joints}} [{{rest_joints_lo}}, {{rest_joints_hi}}] | {{anc_joints}} [{{anc_joints_lo}}, {{anc_joints_hi}}] |
| policy identity | {{rest_policy}} [{{rest_policy_lo}}, {{rest_policy_hi}}] | — |

![Table @tab:controls as a dot plot. Left: at rest, with the 95 % CI. Right: at the grasp anchor, with the seed range (not a CI).](fig/table2.pdf){#fig:controls}

> **Finding.** At rest our model is level with the colour judge. The two CIs overlap. Our model is above the frozen encoder and the joints. At the grasp anchor no judge applies, and our model is above both controls. The pose probes hold the joints better than our encoder does. They hold the outcome worse. On these rollouts the encoder has outcome information that the pose alone does not give.

The anchor column depends on how the five seeds are combined. If we average the predictions before scoring, our model gets {{anc_W1_avg}}, frozen DINOv2-S {{anc_W2p_avg}} and the joints {{anc_joints_avg}}. With seed 0 alone they get {{anc_W1_s0}}, {{anc_W2p_s0}} and {{anc_joints_s0}}. The gap to the frozen encoder is smaller with seed 0. The order is the same.

On SURVEYOR's scale check the controls do better than our model. Frozen DINOv2-S has τ {{surv_tau_W2p}} and the joints τ {{surv_tau_joints}}, and both pass. Our model has τ {{surv_tau_W1}} and fails. SURVEYOR reports {{surv_paper}}. The task and the rollouts are the same for all three features. We take the failure as a property of our latent space.

The first scoring of the outcome readout reports {{g10_rest}}. That number was scored against the colour judge's labels on {{g10_n}} rollouts. Scored against the author's labels on the {{n}} rollouts, the same readout gives {{rest_W1}}.

## Where the predictor fails

The predictor does predict its own latent. At K = 30 its open-loop error is {{g0_err}}, and copying the last latent gives {{g0_copy}}. The uses of this prediction fail. In chunk ranking [@maes2026lewm] the model puts the executed chunk first in {{g1b_top1}} of anchors, against a threshold of {{g1b_bar}}. The commanded actions alone reach {{g1b_actions}}. The surprise monitor catches {{w1a_hits}} failures. The hierarchical planner [@zhang2026hwm] and the risk head [@liu2026reap] also miss their thresholds. So do the conformal surprise band, re-ranking and the calibrated reflex. Table @tab:register gives the margins.

Neither result is luck. Against copy-last the paired difference in latent error is {{bs_g0}} in favour of the model. In chunk ranking the model's top-1 minus the commanded actions' is {{bs_rank_worst}} and {{bs_rank_best}} over {{bs_rank_eps}} validation episodes: the interval is below zero at every distance we scored. Predicting well is not ranking well. The pretrained RoboJEPA predictor also beats copy-last, by less than its claim asks: its 9-step latent error is {{rj_red}} below copy-last (interval {{rj_lo}} to {{rj_hi}}, {{rj_eps}} episodes), short of the {{rj_bar}} its claim asks, and its encoder takes {{rj_ms}} for the two views on our on-board computer against a 200 ms budget.

ContactGuard [@zheng2026contactguard] finds that probes on the imagined latent beat probes on the current latent for grasp tasks (AUROC {{cg_theirs}} on its cup task). We expected the same at our grasp anchor, and the threshold fixed before the test asked the imagined latent for {{c1_bar}} over the current one. We found the reverse. With the same encoder and the same anchor, the current latent reads the outcome at {{c1_current}} and the imagined latent at {{c1_imagined}}. The paired difference is {{bs_cg}} over {{bs_cg_n}} rollouts, so the loss is not luck. The row fails. ContactGuard used one policy and three cameras; we used mixed policies and two.

Two monitors flag failures from the predictor. FAIL-Detect [@xu2025faildetect] and SAFE [@gu2025safe] set a conformal band on a failure score from successful episodes; we used our model's surprise as the score, not their scores, so this is our variant. Our first band was built wrong, calibrated on the same episodes it was fitted on. With the band built as the papers build it ({{cf_cal}} + {{cf_cal}} successes to build the band, medians over {{cf_splits}} random splits), {{cf_fp}} of held-out successes are flagged at α = 5 % (not {{cf_fp_old}}) and {{cf_tp}} of failures are caught (not {{cf_tp_old}}); both still miss. ReAP [@liu2026reap] learns a risk head on the latent and the planned actions. Our first version on our latent caught {{reap_v2}}; rebuilt from the paper with its Stage 1 detector and Stage 2 predictor (no code is released, one seed), it caught {{reap_caught}} of 8 at {{reap_fi}} of 42 false interventions. Both miss 5 of 8. The risk head reads the true actions better than shuffled ones ({{bs_reap_m}} against {{bs_reap_b}} AUROC), but the interval of the difference, {{bs_reap}}, contains zero, so we cannot tell.

We also ran the distillation probe on our model's inverse-dynamics term, pooled over the {{g5_val}} teleoperation validation episodes. The trained model scores {{g5_w1}}. Its random initialisation scores {{g5_rand}}. The difference is {{g5_diff}}, with a 95 % paired episode-bootstrap CI of [{{g5_lo}}, {{g5_hi}}]. The term depends on the machine. The same untrained network scored {{g5_unstable}} on one machine and {{g5_rand}} and {{g5_orin}} on two others. It is sensitive to the BLAS library and to ridge conditioning.

The surprise monitor has a failure-class AUROC of {{m1_auroc}}, below its sanity bar. We checked it for a loading or scoring bug. The same scorer reproduces the open-loop corruption ratios at K = 30. Cross-episode gives {{m1_xep}}, against {{m1_xep_g0}} in the original run. Constant-first gives {{m1_cf}}, against {{m1_cf_g0}}. We found no bug.

We ran an action-shuffle check on the predictor of VLA-JEPA at {{m3_n}} anchors (Table @tab:vlajepa). Action tokens from another episode differ from the true ones by {{m3_tok_lo}}–{{m3_tok_hi}} % in relative L2. They move the loss by about {{m3_sh}} %. Zeroing the tokens moves it by about {{m3_zero}} % on average. The forward pass is deterministic. The predictor barely uses its actions. Its failure-class AUROC is {{m3_auroc}}, below the same sanity check, and we leave its table unread under the frozen bar.

Table @tab:vlajepa: VLA-JEPA world-model loss at {{m3_n}} anchors with true, shuffled (Δ shuffled) and zeroed action tokens (exploratory spot check). The token change is the relative L2 of the shuffled tokens.

| anchor (episode / frame) | true loss | Δ shuffled (%) | token change (%) | Δ zeroed (%) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| {{m3_0_ep}} / {{m3_0_fr}} | {{m3_0_true}} | {{m3_0_sh}} | {{m3_0_tok}} | {{m3_0_zero}} |
| {{m3_1_ep}} / {{m3_1_fr}} | {{m3_1_true}} | {{m3_1_sh}} | {{m3_1_tok}} | {{m3_1_zero}} |
| {{m3_2_ep}} / {{m3_2_fr}} | {{m3_2_true}} | {{m3_2_sh}} | {{m3_2_tok}} | {{m3_2_zero}} |
| {{m3_3_ep}} / {{m3_3_fr}} | {{m3_3_true}} | {{m3_3_sh}} | {{m3_3_tok}} | {{m3_3_zero}} |
