The outcome-readout gate has one pass that uses the imagined latent. In its anticipation step the imagined latent beat policy identity by {{g10_anticip}}. We set that bar after the result, so it is exploratory. We then reran the idea twice with the threshold committed first. One run was the grasp probe and the other the calibrated surprise reflex. Both fail.

## Does more data change this?

All of this comes from one model trained on {{train_eps}} episodes. We trained the same recipe at three other sizes, for the same {{sc_steps}} gradient steps as the original model, and ran the same probes on each checkpoint (Table @tab:scale). The smallest model sees 45 of the {{train_eps}} episodes. The original model is the second row and was not retrained. The third adds {{scale_eps}} episodes of a second cubes-and-bowls dataset of ours at the same frame rate. The fourth adds the 2,099 policy rollouts of ArmnetBench, which come from another lab with other cameras and which we kept at their native 20 Hz, so the step to the last row changes the data and the setup together. The probes, thresholds and decision rule were fixed before any of these runs.

Table @tab:scale: The probe battery on one model per training-set size. Chunk ranking is the top-1 of 8 candidates at the longest horizon (d = 30) with its gap to the commanded-actions ranker ({{sc_floor30}} top-1 on the same anchors) and the 95 % episode-bootstrap CI of the paired gap. The beyond-state columns come from a ridge probe that predicts the commanded action from the latent now and 5 frames later: the first number is its residual R² above the latent now alone or paired with another episode's latent, the second is what the two latents add to the measured joints. The surprise monitor column is the failures caught of 8 at 2 of 42 false interventions. The rest readout is scored on the {{n}} rollouts against the author's labels.

| training episodes (frames) | joints R² | chunk ranking, d = 30 | beyond-state gain / over joints | surprise monitor | rest readout AUROC [CI] |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| {{sc1_eps}} ({{sc1_frames}}) | {{sc1_r2}} | {{sc1_d30}}, gap {{sc1_gap}} [{{sc1_gap_lo}}, {{sc1_gap_hi}}] | {{sc1_p3}} / {{sc1_p3v}} | {{sc1_p4}} of 8 | {{sc1_p5}} [{{sc1_p5_lo}}, {{sc1_p5_hi}}] |
| {{sc3_eps}} ({{sc3_frames}}) | {{sc3_r2}} | {{sc3_d30}}, gap {{sc3_gap}} [{{sc3_gap_lo}}, {{sc3_gap_hi}}] | {{sc3_p3}} / {{sc3_p3v}} | {{sc3_p4}} of 8 | {{sc3_p5}} [{{sc3_p5_lo}}, {{sc3_p5_hi}}] |
| {{sc4_eps}} ({{sc4_frames}}) | {{sc4_r2}} | {{sc4_d30}}, gap {{sc4_gap}} [{{sc4_gap_lo}}, {{sc4_gap_hi}}] | {{sc4_p3}} / {{sc4_p3v}} | {{sc4_p4}} of 8 | {{sc4_p5}} [{{sc4_p5_lo}}, {{sc4_p5_hi}}] |
| {{sc5_eps}} ({{sc5_frames}}) | {{sc5_r2}} | {{sc5_d30}}, gap {{sc5_gap}} [{{sc5_gap_lo}}, {{sc5_gap_hi}}] | {{sc5_p3}} / {{sc5_p3v}} | {{sc5_p4}} of 8 | {{sc5_p5}} [{{sc5_p5_lo}}, {{sc5_p5_hi}}] |

At d = 30 the commanded actions rank the executed chunk first at {{sc_floor30}}. Our model is below them at every size by a gap whose CI excludes zero. Its best cell was {{sc1_top1}} with 45 episodes, {{sc3_top1}} with {{train_eps}}, then {{sc4_top1}} and {{sc5_top1}} after we added the cubes dataset and ArmnetBench. The 0.8 threshold is out of reach at all four sizes. We have not tested why the larger sets did worse. The added episodes are other tasks and another rig, and the ranking is scored on the same {{val_eps}} bowls episodes each time, so the added data may take capacity from the task we score. The surprise monitor caught {{sc1_p4}}, {{sc3_p4}}, {{sc4_p4}} and {{sc5_p4}} of 8 failures. It needed 5.

Neither encoder probe moves with the data. The joints R² is {{sc1_r2}} at the smallest size and {{sc5_r2}} at the largest. The rest readout is level with the colour judge ({{sc_judge}}) in every row, since the CI of the paired difference includes zero, although the largest model has the lowest AUROC, {{sc5_p5}}. For the beyond-state probe, the gain of the latent 5 frames later over the latent now alone is at least {{sc_p3_min}}, but what it adds to the measured joints is {{sc_p3_floor}} to {{sc_p3_ceil}} against a threshold of 0.02, and the untrained encoder gains {{sc_random_gain}} from the same extra latent, more than half of any trained one's gain. The verdict is not proven at every size.

This is one run per size with one seed. The decision rule asks for two seeds at the smallest and the largest size, and for a predictor trained on frozen DINOv2-S features at every size as the floor of the surprise monitor. We ran neither. Read it as four runs in which no use of the predictor improves with data, not as a statement about the model class.

## Related work

LeWorldModel [@maes2026lewm] trains the recipe we use end to end from pixels and plans with it in Push-T and in simulation. We tested its two planning uses on our arm, and both fail. LeJEPA's identifiability analysis [@klindt2026lejepa] predicts that SIGReg latents identify the state up to anisotropy. Our pose probes support this in part. DINO-WM [@zhou2024dinowm] plans on frozen DINOv2 [@oquab2023dinov2] patches, and our frozen control uses the same design. We timed V-JEPA 2.1 [@murlabadia2026vjepa21] and RoboJEPA [@zholus2026robojepa] on our on-board computer, and both are too slow. ContactGuard [@zheng2026contactguard] finds imagined-latent probes better than current-latent ones on grasp tasks. On our arm we find the reverse. We also re-tested ReAP's action-conditioned risk [@liu2026reap], the conformal bands of FAIL-Detect [@xu2025faildetect] and SAFE [@gu2025safe] on our surprise score, the hierarchical planners of HWM [@zhang2026hwm] and H-JEPA [@zhang2026hjepa], and SURVEYOR's latent-distance scale [@surveyor2026]. All of them miss here, at the original data scale. SaiVLA-JEPA [@xiao2026saivla] reports that a training-only future-latent head adds {{sai_gain}} points to control on {{sai_eps}} paired episodes. HIJP [@hijp2026] finds LeWM below goal-conditioned behaviour cloning in simulation.

## Limitations

We chose the probes for the encoder and predictor split after the data, so the split is exploratory. They are fitted on {{n}} rollouts that one operator, the author, labelled. The model never saw these rollouts, and every probe is cross-validated by session. To confirm or reject the split we need a fresh set of rollouts scored under a threshold committed first. That takes a day on the arm. The main results come from one model at one data scale. The data-scale runs add three sizes, one run and one seed each. A second seed at the smallest and the largest size, the frozen-DINOv2-S and random-encoder predictors at each size, the 90-episode size and a successes-only variant of the largest were not run. Three controls were not run. The surprise monitor and the imagined arm of the grasp probe would each need a predictor trained on frozen DINOv2-S features or on the joints. Progress monotonicity with the controls would need a reimplementation. The pass of the outcome readout is retrospective: its anticipation bar was set after the result.

## Conclusion

> **Conclusion.** We ran {{rows}} uses on a world model that cost about ${{cost_round}} to train. The encoder can tell a success from a failure after the fact. The predictor does not add to that, and training on up to {{sc5_eps}} episodes did not change it. For ranking chunks the commanded actions were better than the predictor, and they need no model.

